    {
      "ts": 1790242844.481496,
      "status": "done",
      "result": {
        "summary": "Per-thread connections via threading.local keyed by path, and read() skips bad rows and logs the stderr line, as specified. All three acceptance tests are present as unittest methods, and the gate passed (tests-green: OK 1524). I found no blocking issues.",
        "verdict": "approve",
        "comments": [
          {
            "path": "orchestrator/bus.py",
            "line": 89,
            "issue": "get() now takes locked(), which is outside the spec. The lock is reentrant, so no deadlock, but every read() now takes the file lock once per row and readers serialise against writers. Reasonable as the torn-write fix, but worth a human check for latency.",
            "severity": "low"
          },
          {
            "path": "orchestrator/bus.py",
            "line": 50,
            "issue": "_close_db() closes only the calling thread's connections. Connections in other threads stay open until those threads exit or GC runs, so a test that swaps STATE could leave a stale per-path connection in a long-lived thread. The spec allows this.",
            "severity": "low"
          },
          {
            "path": "tests/test_bus.py",
            "line": 63,
            "issue": "The concurrency test only checks that the id is a non-empty str. It does not check other required fields such as status. A regression that returned a partial dict would pass. It also does not assert the skip path was never hit.",
            "severity": "low"
          }
        ],
        "packet_version": "fc110f0bdb07",
        "confidence": 0.0,
        "provenance": [
          "repo"
        ]
      }
